umn from a different seed becomes colour 2.\n- The demonstrations confirm this:\n  - Pair 1: row 5 (colour 3) meets column 3 (colour 4) at (5,3), which is 2. Row 10 (colour 4) meets column 9 (colour 3) at (10,9), which is 2.\n  - Pair 3, row 2 (colour 3): it crosses column 2 (colour 5) and column 7 (colour 7), and both cells are 2. At column 11, the seed's own column, the cell stays 3.\n  - The counts fit as well: pair 3 has 3 seeds, giving 3×2 = 6 cells of colour 2. Pairs 1 and 2 each have 2 seeds, giving 2 cells of colour 2.\n\n**How the program builds the output:**\n1. Find the background (the most common colour) and the seed cells.\n2. Paint each seed's full column in its colour, and remember which seed owns each column cell.\n3. Paint each seed's full row. Where a row cell already belongs to a different seed's column, write 2 instead.\n4. Emit a canvas after the columns are drawn and again after the rows.\n\nThe program never uses fixed row or column numbers, so the 15×15 size does not matter. The test grid has seeds 8 at (2,3), 4 at (6,10) and 1 at (12,6). The program draws columns 3, 10 and 6, then rows 2, 6 and 12. That gives six crossing cells of colour 2: (2,6), (2,10), (6,3), (6,6), (12,3) and (12,10).","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    seeds = [(y, x, int(g[y, x])) for y in range(H) for x in range(W) if g[y, x] != bg]\n    out = np.full_like(g, bg)\n    owner = -np.ones((H, W), dtype=int)\n    for i, (y, x, c) in enumerate(seeds):\n        out[:, x] = c\n        owner[:, x] = i\n    emit(out, \"Draw a full vertical line through each seed in its colour.\")\n    for i, (y, x, c) in enumerate(seeds):\n        for xx in range(W):\n            if owner[y, xx] >= 0 and owner[y, xx] != i:\n                out[y, xx] = 2\n            else:\n                out[y, xx] = c\n    emit(out, \"Draw each seed's horizontal line; crossings with another seed's column become 2.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"selection":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Find the background (the most common colour) and the seed cells."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"- The counts fit as well: pair 3 has 3 seeds, giving 3×2 = 6 cells of colour 2."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"a33aca250b0500cac19de6419541ed245529311235aa6973e37e5b5420aadcb3","task_sha256":"78bf059c3702e38584be94d8c83630c0a18e7b4a7401ac702b4c053fe32ac7a4","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
